// src/v4/lens/testgen.dag
//
// Scope: testgen lens — reads substrate declarations and produces TestClaim
//        instances structurally. The producer side of "tests are data, not
//        code" (THESIS facet 3). Output is consumed by lens/coverage.dag
//        which verifies the produced set covers the expected substrate
//        cross-product.
// Anchor: TESTING.md §"Test layers (target ratios)" + THESIS.md §348-368
//         "Tests are structural data" + §168-182 correctness tiers +
//         memory: feedback_groundedness_gates_lenses (testgen applies
//         to every program by construction)
//
// Owns:
//   - Generator<C> generic carrier (parameterized over substrate concept)
//   - Per-substrate-kind testgen rules. Every rule emits TestClaims whose
//     `kind` is one of std/verification.dag's CLOSED AssertKind
//     (Equals | Diagnostic | Compiles | RoundTrips) — testgen names the
//     AssertKind, it does NOT coin parallel claim vocabulary:
//     - type_construction(Atom | Conj | Disj | Arrow | Cardinality
//       | Instantiation) -> Set<Compiles TestClaim>
//       (the complete closed 6-connective kernel — no "...")
//     - algebra_law(Algebra × Inhabitant × Law) -> Set<Equals TestClaim>
//     - diagnostic_exhaustiveness(NamedReason variant) -> Set<Diagnostic TestClaim>
//     - lens_applicability(Lens × Program) -> Set<Equals TestClaim>
//     - bidirectional_roundtrip(Language × GrammarProduction) -> Set<RoundTrips TestClaim>
//   - Simulator-generation arm (operator-ratified 2026-05-15: lives HERE,
//     not a separate lens/simulation.dag — testgen owns both claim
//     production and the simulators its boundary claims need):
//     - simulator_generation(WireContract | Effect | Language) -> Node
//       generates a hermetic simulator (just Node — bounded kernel) from
//       the declared contract. NOT hand-written (would be a rotting
//       mirror — feedback_isomorphism_or_generation_for_mirrors); the
//       simulator is structurally bound to the contract and regenerates
//       when it changes.
//     - external_conformance(Language | WireContract × Node)
//       -> Set<{Compiles|Equals|RoundTrips} TestClaim>
//       boundary claims whose `input` is (program ∘ generated simulator) —
//       hermetic AND deterministic. The live oracle (real rustc / live
//       endpoint) is the OPT-IN boundary run, never the default suite.
//   - Simulation adds NO AssertKind: it substitutes generated-simulator
//     Node into `input` at the WireContract boundary. AssertKind stays
//     the closed 4 (see std/verification.dag).
//   - Sim fidelity caveat: a generated simulator is faithful-to-CONTRACT,
//     not faithful-to-reality. Catching contract under-specification is
//     STRUCTURAL — the opt-in live-oracle reconciliation is a CI-as-data
//     gate; sim-pass + oracle-fail ⟹ the WireContract is incomplete ⟹
//     STOP and extend the carrier. Generated simulation is necessary,
//     not sufficient.
//   - TestClassification = (Tier, Layer) for every produced claim
//     - Tier: Tier1 | Tier2 | Tier3 (THESIS correctness tier)
//     - Layer: Unit | Integration | Boundary (TESTING.md test layer)
//   - lens read: () -> Set<TestClaim>  (the produced corpus)
//
// C5-1 / C5-3 (operator-ratified 2026-05-16) — the bidirectional_roundtrip
// contract:
//   - C5-1: a RoundTrips TestClaim asserts `ingest∘emit = id_Node` —
//     B1 `content_hash(N) == content_hash(ingest(emit(N)))` — the
//     MEANING round-trips. It does NOT assert text-byte identity
//     (`emit∘ingest = id_text`); the lexical skin (0x1F vs 31,
//     whitespace, comment placement) is allowed to canonicalize.
//   - C5-3: F (the lossless core a roundtrip claim covers) = the
//     SPEC-PROVABLE meaning set. If the language spec does not
//     unambiguously place a feature on the meaning side it is OUT of F
//     — Declared-normalized (emit canonicalizes; recorded per-feature)
//     or Fail-closed (ingest can't disambiguate without semantics ⇒
//     Diagnostic, never a guess). Conservative / fail-safe: the spec
//     decides, the modeler has NO discretion. A language's roundtrip
//     corpus covers its declared F; non-F shrinkage (e.g. C++
//     most-vexing-parse) is explicit up front, not discovered at test
//     time.
//   - Text-level fidelity (`emit∘ingest = id_text`) is a DEFERRED,
//     ORTHOGONAL future layer: a concrete-syntax/trivia sidecar
//     OUTSIDE the Node and OUTSIDE content_hash, partial (only
//     ingest-originated Nodes; vacuous for synthesized ones). It does
//     NOT change C5-1 or content_hash (Node stays the α-/rename-
//     invariant identity, K-1). The C5-fidelity Declared-normalized
//     per-feature dispositions ARE its forward hook — adopting
//     node-level + the disposition table preserves text-level as a
//     clean future add with zero rework. Not built now.
//
// Tier × Layer cross-cut discipline (per TESTING.md ratio targets):
//   - Unit tests target ~75%: type-construction, exhaustiveness, lens-applicability
//   - Integration tests target ~15%: cross-stage invariants, fixed-point convergence
//   - Boundary tests target ~10%: language roundtrips (rustc/python/go/cpp/ts), L4 runtime equivalence
//   - testgen output respecting these ratios is structurally enforceable via coverage lens
//
// Bootstrap path:
//   - test/claim/manual/ holds hand-authored TestClaims authored after T-1 lands
//   - testgen (T-19) produces the same corpus programmatically
//   - Assertion: testgen-produced set ⊇ manual set; manual claims are
//     anti-regression anchors against testgen drift
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag (substrate to walk)
//   - std/algebra.dag (algebra inhabitance for L7-class testgen)
//   - std/verification.dag (TestClaim output schema)
//   - std/diagnostic.dag (Diagnostic.NamedReason for exhaustiveness testgen)
//   - lens/*.dag (each lens's declared read shape, for applicability testgen)
//   - extdeps/languages/*.dag (bidirectional roundtrip testgen)
//   - extdeps/coordination.dag (WireContract/Effect — simulator-generation arm)
//
// Status: T-19 modeled 2026-05-16 (still-deer-194) — Generator<C> carrier,
//         five category subject types, TestClassification, and rule
//         signatures. Executable fold bodies + simulator arm land with
//         compiler/05_eval.dag (T-22) and coverage (T-18) integration.
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md. T-19 is Phase 1.5 — right after std/*
// substrate (T-1/T-2/T-3) lands, BEFORE compiler stages (T-6+) start.
// This buys test evidence early — every Phase 2+ task gets a testgen-
// derived corpus instead of hand-authoring.

module v4.lens.testgen


import v4.std.node { Connective, Node, Symbol }
import v4.std.verification { TestClaim }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// PRODUCER LENS. This file owns the GENERIC CARRIER and the CLOSED FIVE-
// category subject vocabulary testgen folds over. std/verification.dag
// owns TestClaim SHAPE only (T-3). The five categories are NOT parallel
// AssertKind extensions — each maps INTO the closed four (Equals |
// Diagnostic | Compiles | RoundTrips) per the file header ledger.
//
// Generator<C> mirrors lens/coverage.dag's Coverage<C> pattern: one
// record parameterized by the substrate slice `C` under generation. The
// common case instantiates `C` as `TestgenConcept` below (the disjoint
// union of the five category focal payloads). Specialization to a single
// category is `Generator<TypeConstructionSubject>` etc.
//
// Rule functions are DECLARED here with their authoritative signatures;
// their bodies are substrate folds over declarations (T-22 evaluates
// claims; the fold implementation is intentionally not cemented in Rust
// templates — see project spirit / THESIS migration discipline).


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TestClassification — THESIS correctness tier × TESTING.md test layer.
// Every testgen-emitted claim carries this cross-cut for coverage ratio
// enforcement (lens/coverage.dag T-18 reads the same axes).
//
// Coproduct dissolution: both enums are 🟢 terminal closed classifiers;
// this record is a Conj — no ledger.
type TestgenTier
  = Tier1
  | Tier2
  | Tier3


type TestgenLayer
  = Unit
  | Integration
  | Boundary


type TestClassification {
  tier: TestgenTier
  layer: TestgenLayer
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Generator<C> — generic carrier: one scheduled generation unit over a
// substrate concept slice `C`. `classification` is the Tier×Layer tag
// applied to every TestClaim this unit contributes to the corpus.
//
// Coproduct dissolution: Conj record — no ledger.
type Generator<C> {
  classification: TestClassification
  slot: C
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Five category SUBJECT types — inputs to the five testgen rules (the
// substrate concepts testgen reads). Symbols are K-1 name-references to
// declarations in their owning dimensions (never minted at use sites).


// Closed six-connective kernel slice for type-construction testgen
// (Compiles claims — one connective variant per generated claim family).
type TypeConstructionSubject {
  connective: Connective
}


// Algebra × inhabitant × law — each field is a declared Symbol in the
// namespace of std/algebra.dag or the inhabiting concept file (L7).
type AlgebraLawSubject {
  algebra: Symbol
  inhabitant: Symbol
  law: Symbol
}


// One diagnostic-reason symbol exhaustiveness cell (the reason set is
// owned per dimension, not enumerated in std/diagnostic.dag — this cell
// names WHICH declared reason variant the Diagnostic claim targets).
type DiagnosticExhaustivenessSubject {
  reason: Symbol
}


// Lens applicability: declared lens module + program under test.
type LensApplicabilitySubject {
  lens: Symbol
  program: Node
}


// Bidirectional roundtrip anchor: language + grammar production symbols.
type BidirectionalRoundtripSubject {
  language: Symbol
  production: Symbol
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TestgenConcept — the closed disjoint union of the five category subjects.
// `Generator<TestgenConcept>` is the unified scheduling carrier; coverage
// may also read this sum when checking combinatorial completeness.
type TestgenConcept
  = TypeConstruction { subject: TypeConstructionSubject }
  | AlgebraLaw { subject: AlgebraLawSubject }
  | DiagnosticExhaustiveness { subject: DiagnosticExhaustivenessSubject }
  | LensApplicability { subject: LensApplicabilitySubject }
  | BidirectionalRoundtrip { subject: BidirectionalRoundtripSubject }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Rule I/O contracts (authoritative signatures — bodies are substrate folds,
// not enumerated claim matrices; land with T-22 eval + T-18 coverage):
//   fn type_construction(subject: TypeConstructionSubject) -> Set<TestClaim>
//   fn algebra_law(subject: AlgebraLawSubject) -> Set<TestClaim>
//   fn diagnostic_exhaustiveness(subject: DiagnosticExhaustivenessSubject)
//        -> Set<TestClaim>
//   fn lens_applicability(subject: LensApplicabilitySubject) -> Set<TestClaim>
//   fn bidirectional_roundtrip(subject: BidirectionalRoundtripSubject)
//        -> Set<TestClaim>
